    "summary": "Scorching Candyman, fiery Day Tripper & haunting He's Gone→Miracle",
    "blurb": "The show bursts open with a high‑energy \"The Music Never Stopped\" and delivers standout performances of Candyman, Iko Iko and an extended He’s Gone→Miracle sequence. The second set builds into a thunderous drum jam that feeds into a surprise Day Tripper finish. Overall the band is locked in, with Jerry’s vocals and guitar soaring throughout.",
    "review": "Portland‑ME, March 31 1985 – this Dead show is often cited as one of the spring ’85 highlights. The first set rides a tight groove, kicking off with \"The Music Never Stopped\" and moving through a punchy Candyman that showcases Garcia’s powerful vocals and soaring tone. Mid‑set gems like \"C.C. Rider\" and \"Loser\" keep the momentum rolling, while the crowd gets a treat from the unexpected \"Iko Iko\" segue into \"Samson & Delilah\" in the second set.\n\nThe real fireworks erupt after the He’s Gone→Miracle transition, where Jerry stays onstage through an extended drums‑space and guides the band into a seamless jam that culminates in a blistering \"Day Tripper\" cover. The Wheel and Playing In The Band provide expansive improvisational space, highlighted by Bill and Mickey’s tribal beats and Brent’s solid keyboard foundation. Even the encore’s \"U.S. Blues\" lands with the same joyous energy. Across multiple audience and soundboard sources the performance is consistently praised as “on fire,” making this a must‑listen for any Deadhead looking for that 1985 spark.",
